# Sync with origin before you build

Work on this repo happens from more than one session and machine. On
2026-11-05 a push was rejected because the iOS 2.6.1 release commits (build 49)
had landed on `origin/main` from another session. Building or shipping from a
stale checkout would have left them out.

## Do this first, every time

Before any of these: `npm run build`, `vercel`/a production deploy, an iOS
archive or `scripts/ship-b*.sh` or `fastlane ios ship`, a version or build-number
bump, or `git push`:

```sh
git fetch origin
git status -sb          # look for "behind"
```

If the branch is behind:

```sh
git pull --rebase --autostash origin main
```

Then re-run the typecheck (`npx tsc --noEmit` in `chorestar-nextjs/`) on the
merged tree before building. A clean rebase does not mean the merge compiles.

## Rules

- Never `git push --force` or `--force-with-lease` to `main` to get past a
  rejected push. Fetch, rebase, push.
- Check overlap first: `git diff --name-only HEAD...origin/main`. If the
  incoming commits touch files you also changed, read them before rebasing.
- If the rebase conflicts, stop and show the conflicting files. Do not resolve
  conflicts in release or billing code (`ChoreStar-iOS/`, `app/api/stripe`,
  `app/api/apple`, `app/api/google`, `lib/billing`) without asking.
- Bumping `APP_BUILD` or the version: do it only after the pull, so build
  numbers cannot collide with one already on `origin/main` or in App Store
  Connect (`node ChoreStar-iOS/scripts/asc.mjs builds`).
- Tell the user what came in (commit subjects) whenever the pull was not empty.
